{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nADR 0122 fixed the retrieval architecture but left the index unbuilt. This story builds it on a tier needing no model provider, credentials or network, so retrieval works in every installation. Siblings add the embedding ranking, the create advisory, and the ADR amendments.\n\n## Approach\n\n### Ranking\n\nsqlite FTS5 over title and description, bm25 weights title 5.0 / description 1.0 — ADR 0122's measured `fts5` baseline (`evals/similar-tickets/measure.py:389-396`, weights `\"0.0, 5.0, 1.0\"`). Semantic recall@5 0.823 vs the adopted `vector`'s 0.876; it wins at lexical recall@10 (0.993 vs 0.972, p=0.0000) — ADR 0122's reason for keeping it. Query text is title plus the first 200 characters of description, per ADR 0122.\n\nResults cap at 5: ADR 0122's decision is scoped to k=5, and at k=10 `vector`'s edge is indistinguishable (p=0.3438), where the evidence favours building nothing. A different count invalidates the comparison.\n\nThe query API excludes a caller-supplied ticket id: a create queries with its own new ticket's text, already indexed by catch-up, so without the exclusion it gets itself back as top match.\n\n`id`, `alias` and `title` are stored columns on the index row, so serving a query needs no ticket-store read. `score` is computed per query and normalized to 0–1, 1 most relevant: raw bm25 is negative and ranks ascending, inverting the conventional reading and leaving consumers unable to assert on it.\n\n### Persistence and freshness\n\nA sqlite file under `.rebar/cache/similar-tickets/`, mirroring the git-ignored cache at `.rebar/cache/plan-review/` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/checkpoints.py:33-37`). `.gitignore:23` ignores `.rebar/*` wholesale, so no ignore change is needed and nothing derived reaches the `tickets` branch.\n\nThe index persists a map from ticket directory to its last-indexed dir hash; that map makes catch-up cheap and new tickets discoverable. `compute_dir_hash(ticket_dir, event_filenames)` (`src/rebar/reducer/_cache.py:122`) supplies the per-directory hash only — it detects change in a known directory but cannot discover a new one. Catch-up therefore runs two steps:\n\n1. **Enumerate** the live ticket-directory set from the store.\n2. **Diff** it against the persisted map. No entry means new, so index it. A differing hash means changed, so re-index it. A missing directory means gone, so drop the entry.\n\nStep 1 is what makes a just-created ticket discoverable by the next query.\n\nCatch-up is synchronous, in the read phase before serving a query — no daemon, background thread or CI trigger — so behaviour is identical on a fresh clone, under MCP, and where no CI provider exists.\n\n### Applying the diff, and why there is no rebuild\n\nThe diff is applied **in place, to the live index, inside one sqlite transaction** — never built into a replacement database. That is why unchanged entries survive: reconciliation touches only the rows the diff named. Building a fresh database from the diff alone would discard every unchanged entry; copying the live file first would pay the store's full size on every create.\n\nsqlite supplies the atomicity and crash-safety, so no temporary file and no `os.rename` swap appears — removing the POSIX-versus-Windows rename-overwrite difference as a concern, since neither call is used. The index opens in WAL mode with a busy timeout; each reconciliation wraps its writes in `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` … `COMMIT`, so an interrupted process rolls back and leaves the previous committed state intact. Concurrent reconcilers serialize on sqlite's write lock; a loser's work is redone, not merged, since each recomputes the hashes it writes. Measured on this checkout — see the ticket comment.\n\nA reader opening a corrupt index discards and rebuilds it, so the cache is regenerable and needs no eviction path.\n\nOn an unchanged store every hash matches its stored value, so the diff is empty, no transaction opens and no FTS5 write occurs. Warm cost is one enumeration plus one stat per event file.\n\n### Failure disposition\n\nEvery failure path returns an empty result rather than raising, so retrieval can never fail a caller. Each swallowed failure logs at warning level first; without it a corrupt index degrades to permanently empty results with no observable symptom.\n\nNo new config key is added; any ratcheted mechanism introduced carries a `# mechanism-ok:` marker naming this ticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A query returns at most 5 results. Checked by: a test over a store with more matches.\n- [ ] Each result carries `id`, `alias`, `title` and `score`. Checked by: a shape test over one result.\n- [ ] Every `score` lies in the closed interval 0 to 1. Checked by: a test asserting the range.\n- [ ] The top-ranked result holds the maximum `score` of the returned set. Checked by: a test asserting the ordering.\n- [ ] A caller-supplied excluded ticket id never appears in that query's results. Checked by: a test querying with a ticket's own text and id.\n- [ ] Catch-up indexes a ticket directory created after the last build. Checked by: a test creating a directory, running catch-up, then querying.\n- [ ] Catch-up drops index entries whose ticket directory no longer exists. Checked by: a test removing a directory then querying.\n- [ ] Catch-up re-indexes a ticket whose dir hash changed. Checked by: a test mutating a ticket then matching its updated text.\n- [ ] Reconciling a diff naming one ticket leaves every other indexed ticket returnable with its prior text. Checked by: a test indexing 3 tickets, mutating 1, then asserting the other 2 are unchanged.\n- [ ] Catch-up performs no re-indexing work on an unchanged store. Checked by: a test asserting the re-indexed count is 0 on a second run.\n- [ ] A warm query completes within a p95 of 500 ms over at least 20 runs. Checked by: a timing test over a synthetic store it generates.\n- [ ] That timing test asserts its generated store holds at least 10,000 tickets before measuring. Checked by: the test's precondition assertion.\n- [ ] The cold-catch-up p95 over that same store is emitted as a numeric figure. Checked by: a test asserting the measurement is produced.\n- [ ] A ticket created in-process is returned by the next query in that same process, with no daemon, background thread, MCP server or CI. Checked by: an integration test.\n- [ ] With every model-provider credential environment variable unset, a query returns a non-empty ranked result. Checked by: an integration test clearing those variables.\n- [ ] After 100 writes and 100 queries against a test store, the `tickets` branch contains only user event types. Checked by: an integration test asserting the event-type set.\n- [ ] A query returns an empty result rather than raising when the index file is absent. Checked by: a test asserting the return value.\n- [ ] A corrupt index file is discarded and rebuilt. Checked by: a test corrupting the file then asserting a rebuild.\n- [ ] A query against a rebuilt index returns results. Checked by: a test querying after that rebuild.\n- [ ] A query returns an empty result rather than raising when catch-up raises. Checked by: a test injecting a raising catch-up.\n- [ ] An absent index file emits a warning-level log record. Checked by: a test capturing logs.\n- [ ] A corrupt index file emits a warning-level log record. Checked by: a test capturing logs.\n- [ ] A raising catch-up emits a warning-level log record. Checked by: a test capturing logs.\n- [ ] Two concurrent catch-up runs leave the index passing `PRAGMA integrity_check`. Checked by: a concurrency test running two in parallel.\n- [ ] Both tickets written by those concurrent runs are returnable afterwards. Checked by: the same test querying for each.\n- [ ] Every module added or modified under `src/rebar/` is at most 800 lines. Checked by: the CI module-size gate.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` exits 0. Checked by: running it.\n- [ ] `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exits 0. Checked by: running it."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793582019883242805,"uuid":"09c6f313-db19-4ea1-89a1-53f081e792f2"}